import eliot.collection.List

/**
 * Fold, at compile time, over every top-level value named `name` in the program.
 *
 * The general form of compile-time reflection. Across all modules of the source pool it reifies to
 * `combine(qualifiedName₁, value₁, combine(qualifiedName₂, value₂, initial))` — a right fold in which each gathered
 * value is an ordinary **argument** of `combine`, labelled with its canonical qualified name (`pkg.Module::value`). It
 * is the building block for registries, suites and instance tables gathered from many modules without a central list
 * anyone must remember to edit: each module simply declares a value under the agreed name, and the fold finds it.
 *
 * Because a gathered value is handed to a *declared slot* instead of being stored, it is `combine` that decides what it
 * receives. A slot with a `uses` clause receives the gathered value as code to run in place; an entry the slot
 * *supplies* is one `combine` discharges; a `with` on the slot's clause entry binds the gathered value's calls to an
 * implementation of `combine`'s choosing. That is what `namedValues` cannot do — a list element is a payload, and a
 * payload may never be a computation — so an effectful value can be gathered here and not there. Each value is
 * monomorphized on its own, so the gathered values may differ in the effects they use *and* in type, as long as each one fits
 * `combine`'s slot; a value that does not is a compile error, never a silent omission.
 *
 * `name` must be a literal `String`, and `combine` must be a **declared value** rather than a lambda, since only a
 * declaration can state the `uses` clauses of the values it receives; both are hard errors rather than an empty fold. The
 * values are folded in qualified-name order, so a suite runs the same way on every build.
 *
 * ```eliot
 * // Every test module declares one of these, and imports nothing from the framework.
 * def test uses Console, Throw[String]: Unit
 *
 * // One test, then the rest of the suite. The failure is discharged here, so a failing test
 * // does not fail the run, and `rest` — every test to the right — is still unrun at this point.
 * def runTest(name: String, test uses *, Throw[String]: Unit, rest uses *: Unit) uses Console: Unit = {
 *    printLine("- " ++ name)
 *    test catch (e -> printLine("  FAILED: " ++ e))
 *    rest
 * }
 *
 * def main uses Console: Unit = foldNamedValues("test", printLine("done"), runTest)
 * ```
 */
def foldNamedValues[B, C](name: String, initial: B, combine: C): B

/**
 * Compile-time reflection over the top-level values of a program, collected into a list.
 *
 * `namedValues[V](name)` reifies, at compile time, to the list of **every top-level value named `name`** in the
 * program's source pool — across all modules — as a `List[V]`. It is `foldNamedValues` at the free monoid, and the
 * shape to reach for whenever the gathered values are plain data; reach for the fold itself when they are
 * computations, which a list cannot hold.
 *
 * ```eliot
 * // Each plugin module declares `def contribution: Int = ...`; this gathers them all.
 * def total: Int = namedValues[Int]("contribution").foldLeft(0, e -> acc -> add(e, acc))
 * ```
 *
 * `name` must be a literal `String` — it drives a compile-time rewrite, so a computed name is a hard error rather than
 * an empty list. `V` is not a filter but a *claim*: the collection asserts each gathered value has type `V`, and the
 * ordinary type checker enforces it, so a same-named value that is not a `V` is a compile error pointing at the call.
 * The result is ordered deterministically (by the values' qualified names). A nullary `def` matches; a value that takes
 * arguments has a function type, not `V`, so it is naturally excluded.
 */
def namedValues[V](name: String): List[V]
